# Part One — Gender-Based Violence

# Chapter 1 · Defining Gender-Based Violence

> Three questions began with a definition: "**Define gender-based violence**" (2023), "**What is gender-based violence?**" (2024), and "What are the different forms of gender-based violence…?" (2017). A 2025 MCQ asked what GBV is (**violence based on sex or gender**), and a 2024 MCQ asked how many women worldwide face it (**1 in 3**).

## 1.1  Violence, gender and gender-based violence

**Violence** is the intentional use of physical force or power, threatened or actual, that results in — or is likely to result in — injury, death, psychological harm, maldevelopment or deprivation (WHO, ==2002==). Notice that the WHO definition includes **power**, not only force, and **deprivation**, not only injury.

**Gender** refers to the roles, behaviour and attributes a society considers appropriate for women and men (Topic 1). **Gender-based violence** joins the two: it is violence **rooted in gender inequality** and in the norms that give men power over women.

::: define Gender-based violence | UNHCR and UN Women
"Harmful acts directed at an individual or a group of individuals **based on their gender**. It is rooted in **gender inequality**, the **abuse of power** and **harmful norms**." It includes physical, sexual, psychological and economic harm, and threats of such acts, in public or private life.
:::

GBV can be directed at men and boys too — for example, sexual violence against men in conflict or violence against gay or transgender people. But because it grows out of unequal power, **women and girls** are its main victims. That is why the terms **GBV** and **violence against women (VAW)** are often used together.

## 1.2  The landmark definition — DEVAW 1993

::: law UN Declaration on the Elimination of Violence against Women (1993) | Articles 1 and 2
**Article 1:** "violence against women" means "any act of gender-based violence that results in, or is likely to result in, **physical, sexual or psychological harm or suffering** to women, including **threats** of such acts, **coercion** or **arbitrary deprivation of liberty**, whether occurring in **public or in private life**."
**Article 2** lists three sites: (a) violence in the **family** (battering, sexual abuse of girls, dowry violence, marital rape, female genital mutilation and other traditional practices); (b) violence in the **community** (rape, sexual harassment at work and in schools, trafficking, forced prostitution); and (c) violence **perpetrated or condoned by the State**.
-> The definition every examiner expects. Note its four parts: **harm** (physical, sexual, psychological), **threats and coercion**, **deprivation of liberty**, and **public or private** life.
:::

The **General Assembly** adopted DEVAW on ==20 December 1993==, after the **Vienna World Conference on Human Rights** (==1993==) declared that "the human rights of women… are an inalienable, integral and indivisible part of universal human rights". In ==1999== the UN designated **25 November** as the **International Day for the Elimination of Violence against Women**, which begins the global "**16 Days of Activism**" ending on Human Rights Day, 10 December.

## 1.3  Other international milestones

::: fig timeline | How violence against women became an international human-rights issue.
@layout vertical
1979: CEDAW adopted — violence not yet named
1992: CEDAW General Recommendation 19 — GBV is discrimination
1993: Vienna Conference; DEVAW adopted (20 December)
1994: UN Special Rapporteur on violence against women; Belém do Pará Convention (Americas)
1995: Beijing Platform — Critical Area D, "Violence against women"
1999: 25 November named International Day for the Elimination of VAW
2000: UNSCR 1325 — women, peace and security
2011: Istanbul Convention (Council of Europe)
2015: SDG target 5.2 — eliminate all forms of violence against women and girls
2017: CEDAW General Recommendation 35 updates GR 19
:::

- **CEDAW** (==1979==) did not mention violence directly. Its committee filled the gap with **General Recommendation 19** (==1992==), which declared that gender-based violence is a form of **discrimination** that seriously inhibits women's ability to enjoy rights and freedoms. **General Recommendation 35** (==2017==) updated it, recognising that the prohibition of GBV has become a principle of customary international law.
- The **Beijing Platform for Action** (==1995==) made **"violence against women"** one of its twelve critical areas (**Area D**).
- The **Council of Europe's Istanbul Convention** (==2011==) is the most detailed binding treaty, requiring prevention, protection, prosecution and integrated policies — the "**four Ps**".
- **SDG target 5.2** (==2015==) calls for the elimination of all forms of violence against all women and girls, and **5.3** for the elimination of child marriage and other harmful practices.

## 1.4  Pakistan's legal definition

Pakistan has no single national definition of GBV, but the **Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act 2016** gives the most complete one. Section 2(r) defines **violence** as "any offence committed against the human body of the aggrieved person including abetment of an offence, **domestic violence, sexual violence, psychological abuse, economic abuse, stalking or a cybercrime**". It also explains **economic abuse** (denial of food, clothing and shelter, or taking away a woman's income) and **psychological violence** (deterioration such as depression or suicide attempts resulting from oppressive behaviour or restricted movement). Chapter 7 studies the Act in detail.

## 1.5  Types of GBV — four ways to classify

::: fig grid | Four ways to classify gender-based violence.
@cols 2
By nature: physical, sexual, psychological, economic
By site: home, community and workplace, state and custody, online
By perpetrator: partner, family, acquaintance, stranger, state
By life cycle: before birth, childhood, adolescence, adulthood, old age
:::

### (1) By nature

1. **Physical:** slapping, beating, burning, acid attacks, murder.
2. **Sexual:** rape (including marital rape), sexual assault, harassment, forced marriage, trafficking.
3. **Psychological or emotional:** threats, insults, humiliation, isolation, controlling behaviour.
4. **Economic:** denial of money, food or inheritance; taking a woman's earnings; preventing her from working.

### (2) By site

Home (domestic violence), community and workplace (harassment, rape, honour killings ordered by *jirgas*), the **state** (custodial violence, discriminatory laws, police inaction) and **online** (cyber-harassment). Question 2016 asked for the "**sites**" of violence — use this classification.

### (3) By life cycle

WHO and UNICEF describe violence across a woman's life:

::: table | Violence across the life cycle of a woman.
@widths 3,11
Stage | Forms of violence
Before birth | Sex-selective abortion; neglect of pregnant women carrying girls
Infancy and childhood | Neglect of girls, less food and health care; child sexual abuse; child labour
Adolescence | Child and forced marriage; *vani* and *swara*; harassment on the way to school; trafficking
Reproductive years | Domestic violence; marital rape; honour killing; acid attacks; dowry violence; harassment at work
Old age | Neglect; denial of inheritance; abuse of widows
:::

## 1.6  The continuum of violence

The British sociologist **Liz Kelly**, in *Surviving Sexual Violence* (==1988==), argued that violence against women is a **continuum**: everyday harassment, threats, coercion and rape are connected, not separate events. They share a common cause — men's power — and a common effect — women's fear and restricted freedom. The continuum explains why "small" acts such as street harassment matter: they make women limit their movements, just as the threat of rape does.

::: fig spectrum | Kelly's continuum of violence — from everyday to extreme, all connected.
@left Everyday and normalised
@right Extreme and lethal
8: Sexist jokes, staring, catcalling
28: Street and workplace harassment; stalking
48: Control, threats, economic abuse
68: Beating; sexual assault; acid attacks
90: Rape; honour killing; femicide
:::

## 1.7  Why GBV is a human-rights, health and development issue

- **Human rights:** it violates the rights to life, security, dignity, equality and freedom from torture (Pakistan's **Articles 9, 14 and 25**).
- **Public health:** the WHO calls violence against women a "**global public health problem of epidemic proportions**" — causing injury, depression, unwanted pregnancy, HIV and death.
- **Development:** it keeps girls out of school and women out of work, and costs economies through health care, lost output and justice spending.
- **Security and democracy:** fear of violence keeps women out of public life, including politics (Topic 6).

::: balance | GBV or VAW — which term should you use?
- **"Violence against women"** keeps the focus on women, who are the overwhelming majority of victims, and on men's power.
- **"Gender-based violence"** is broader — it includes violence against men and boys and against sexual minorities when it is driven by gender norms, and shows that the cause is **gender**, not biology.
-> **Line to take:** define **GBV** (as the questions ask), then explain that it falls **mainly on women** because it is rooted in unequal gender power; use DEVAW for the definition of **VAW**.
:::

::: eye | The model introduction for 2023 and 2024
"Gender-based violence is harm directed at a person because of their gender, rooted in unequal power between women and men. The UN Declaration of ==1993== defines violence against women as any act of gender-based violence that results in physical, sexual or psychological harm, including threats, coercion or deprivation of liberty, in public or private life. Worldwide about **1 in 3** women experience it; in Pakistan **28%** of ever-married women have suffered physical violence (PDHS 2017–18)."
:::
